# Cab Lab usage log (read before guessing)

When the user reports something that went wrong in The Cab Lab (box placed at the wrong place, wrong size, inference not engaging, handle drag misbehaving, crash, file not opening…), **read the log first**; do not ask them to re-describe coordinates or steps.

## Files (all under `the-cab-lab/logs/`, git-ignored)

1. `latest.json` — last action and last error of the current session. Start here.
2. `usage.jsonl` — append-only event stream, one JSON object per line (`seq`, `t`, `kind`, …). Read the last ~50 lines; the event the user is talking about is normally the last `place.finish` / `handle.end` / `error`.
3. `crash-<time>.json` — full `job.json` + cursor trace written on `window.error` / unhandled rejection.

`Ctrl+Shift+L` in the app opens this folder.

## Event kinds

- `session.start` — version, viewport.
- `place.arm` / `place.anchor` / `place.face` / `place.corner` / `place.typein` / `place.trace` / `place.finish` / `place.repeat` / `place.cancel` / `place.blocked` / `place.unfit` — one box placement in three steps (anchor on a face → opposite corner of a flat rectangle on that face → extrude along the face normal, away from the solid only). Side walls are only pickable from the room inside. `place.anchor` / `place.face` carry `plane` `{axis, value, dir, label}` (`place.face` = a corner anchor switched to the face being drawn over). `place.finish` carries `how` (click / enter / `*.preset` / click.locked / repeat), `anchor`, `corner`, `locked` (typed dims), `plane`, `extrude` (`len`, snap `label`), `clamped` (dims stopped by the space, with the wall name) and the final `box` (x0/y0/z0/W/D/H). `place.trace` is the last 60 cursor samples: `cx/cy` client px, `x/y/z` resolved world mm, `kind` = `feature` | `inference` | `align` | `plane` | `extrude` | `extrude.snap` (with `len`, `label`), `dir`, `shift`.
- `move.start` / `move.kind` / `move.target` / `move.finish` / `move.copy` / `move.cancel` — Move (M). One command, three ways to choose, picked on the card (`kind`): `free` (arrows and rings), `face` (two faces, same direction, the first moves onto the second's plane), `point` (a corner onto another point, no rotation). `target` is `module` or `panel`, same rules as before. `move.kind` is switching that page; it throws away the unconfirmed slide. Face picks log `align.pick` / `align.finish` / `align.blocked`; point picks log `pointalign.pick` / `pointalign.finish` / `pointalign.blocked`. Those are not separate commands and do not make their own undo step — Enter is `move.finish`, Esc is `move.cancel`, one step for the whole Move. Ctrl+Enter copies a module. A module with `attach` (bed box, bed side table) keeps the pose the body gives it — use Panel. A board nudge is `cabinet.overrides.boards[roleId]`. `move.finish` carries `from` / `to` pose, `clamped` when the room stopped a module.
- `orient.start` / `orient.pending` / `orient.blocked` / `orient.end` / `orient.cancel` — the Face command (`face` `{axis, dir, label}` clicked, `from` / `to` `{pose, envelope}` — the box never moves, W/D swap; `blocked` = side against a wall / neighbour, `small` = W/D under the module minimum; `how` = click / enter, `changed`).
- `place.finish` also carries `side` `{axis, dir}` (door side chosen: never against a wall / neighbour, else long edge toward the room), `size` `{W, D, H}` in module terms and the resulting `pose`.
- Overhead (`moduleId: overheadCabinet`, ceiling-hung): `place.anchor` carries `walls` (the ceiling ∩ wall lines the anchor sits on) and `term` (`{x, y, z}` → which module dimension W/D/H runs along each world axis once the back wall is known); `place.face` carries `term` too; `place.finish` carries `wall` (the back wall's label; W runs along it, doors face away from it). `place.blocked` with `reason: "not on a ceiling edge"` = the click was not on a feature point where the flat ceiling meets a space wall, a cabinet's outer box, or a partition (the board stops short of the roof; the feature point is the extension). At a corner the back wall is decided at the end: the wall the longer horizontal edge follows.
- `orient.blocked` with `reason: "module has a fixed door side"` / `key.rotate` with `blocked` — Face and `R` are refused on an overhead. Move still translates and rotates it on X Y Z.
- `ohc.zone.select` / `ohc.zone.drag` (`boundary`, `changed`, `widths` — one boundary drag on the strip, one undo step) / `ohc.zone.type` (`zone`, `type`) / `ohc.zone.width` / `ohc.zone.add` / `ohc.zone.remove` (`removed`) / `ohc.zone.average` (`zones`) / `ohc.zone.blocked` — the overhead editor page (right panel). `widths` always sum to `cabinetWidth`; none is under 150. A "wrong zone" report is normally the last of these plus the `handle.end` of a vertical divider bar (`zones` there lists widths for an overhead).
- `tall.zone.select` (`zone` = the `zoneItem.id` such as `zone-2`, null = deselect) / `tall.zone.drag` (`boundary` `"zone"` with `index` (the gap after zone `index`) or `"dividerCenterX"` with `zone` (x drag of a vertical divider centreline), `from`, `to` (zone `height` list or the centre x), `changed`, `where: "front view"` — one drag, one undo step) / `tall.zone.<kind>` (`zone`, kind `type`|`add`|`remove`|`height`|`appliance`|`divider`|`shelf`|`shelfHeight` — toolbar + zone card edits; `add` puts an open zone on top and takes up to 300 from the tallest zone (`from`), `remove` gives the height to the zone below (`heir`), so the stack total never changes) / `tall.zone.blocked` (`reason` — no zone could give 60). The zone grip sits on the **centre line of the boundary panel** between two zones (`to` is where that centre line went) — the tall-cabinet editor page (`renderTall`, front elevation). The stack is read from `result.stack` (`bottom_system` / `zoneItem` / `boundary_panel` / `top_system`); a zone's z range is the row's `z0`/`z1`. A "wrong zone height" report is the last `tall.zone.drag` plus the zone's `height` in `cabinet.params`.
- `kitchen.cell.select` (`column` index, `zone` id such as `zone-1`, null = deselect) / `kitchen.cell.drag` (`boundary` `"column"` with `index` (the boundary after column `index`) or `"zone"` with `column` + `index` (the boundary after zone `index`, top-down), `to` = the resulting column `width` list or that column's zone `height` list, `changed`, `where: "front view"` — the neighbour column/zone absorbs the difference, one undo step) / `kitchen.cell.<kind>` (`column`, `zone` — toolbar + cell card edits: `type` `height` `width` `shelf` `lock`; `zoneAdd` splits the selected zone — a drawer above it takes up to 200 (`from`); `zoneRemove` gives the height to the zone below/above (`heir`); `add` a column on the right taking up to half of the widest (`from`, `widths`); `columnRemove` gives the width to the neighbour (`heir`)) / `kitchen.cell.blocked` (`reason`). A kick-height change re-fits every column's zones to height − kick. Zones inside a column are stored **top-down** (zones[0] is the highest, z1 = H), and a column width drag keeps the order and total length. Resolved cell rectangles come from `result.debug.columns` (generator-resolved `x0/x1`, `z0/z1` — never recomputed in the panel). The generator can emit a valid cabinet **with** `validation.errors` (e.g. the documented V1 half-slot conflict) — the front view still renders; a "blank front view" report is boards/columns missing, not an error entry.
- `lounge.run.select` (`run` = footprint key `i` | `main` | `l` | `left` | `right`, null = deselect) / `lounge.run.drag` (`param` mainWidth|mainDepth|lWidth|lDepth|totalWidth|singleLoungeWidth|depth, `from`, `to`, `changed`, `where: "plan view"` — one orange edge drag, one undo step) / `lounge.run.<kind>` (`key`, `to` — kind `size` `side` `lid` `midCab` `wheel`: run card / Shape section edits) / `lounge.run.style` (`from`, `to` — `setStyle` keeps the outer W / D where they mean the same thing and re-seeds the other sizes from the seat depth) — the lounge editor page (`renderLounge`). The view is **top-down** (`footprint` rects, wall at top): a "wrong lounge shape" report is about `footprint`/`openings`/`lids` from the generator, never an elevation. Runs: I = `i`; L = `main` + `l` (wing sits at y0, `lPosition` LEFT|RIGHT); U = `left` + `main` + `right`; PARALLEL = `left` + `right` + optional middle cabinet block (not a footprint run).
- **Lounge L in 3D** (rail Lounge → L, `interact.js` `lshape`): five clicks. Clicks 1–3 are the ordinary box placement (`place.anchor` / `place.face` / `place.corner` with `moduleId: loungeGenerator`) and draw the **main box**; instead of `place.finish` it logs `lounge.box` (`box` x0/y0/z0/W/D/H, `front` `{axis, dir}` = the room face: the long side not against a wall / solid, else toward the room centre, `length` along the wall, `depth`). `place.blocked` `reason: "lounge main box not longer than deep"` = length ≤ depth + 50. Click 4 `lounge.edge` (`end` lo|hi along the wall, `side` LEFT|RIGHT seen from the room = `lPosition`): the nearer vertical edge of the room face. Click 5 pulls the wing out of the room face: `lounge.typein` (`dim: "L"`, `locked`), `lounge.blocked` (wing under 50), `lounge.finish` (`box`, `front`, `end`, `lPosition`, `wing` `{len, snap (true = snapped to the main depth), locked, clamped (wall name / solid id)}`, `params`, `pose`; `how` click | enter | `enter.default` = Enter before pulling → len = main depth). The wing's width along the wall is always the main depth (`lDepth = mainDepth`); `mainWidth` = main box length, `lWidth` = depth + wing len, pose = `fitBoxFacing(L envelope, front, 0)`. `lounge.back` (`step` the one returned to: pull → edge → box). A "wing on the wrong end" report is `lounge.edge` `end`/`side`; "wing too short/long" is `lounge.finish` `wing`. The floor-plan Lounge tool still uses the older corner / face / side / width flow (`lounge.place.*`).
- `retype.start` / `retype.typein` / `retype.end` / `retype.cancel` — digits re-sizing the last created box while still armed.
- `nose.arm` / `nose.drag` / `nose.typein` / `nose.finish` / `nose.cancel` — Bedroom (nose slab) placement: `arm` carries `depth` (preset 700 or the existing slab's) and `editId` (non-null = the module was picked again and the existing slab is being re-sized, nothing is duplicated); `drag` = the click that started the room-side face following the cursor; `finish` carries `how` (click / enter), `depth`, `locked`, `snap` (label), `clamped` (`back wall` / `minimum N` / a cabinet id), `edited`, `changed`, `pose` (always `rotZ 180`, `y = depth`), `envelope` and the bound `roofProfile` (local Y from the room face). Width / height of the slab are never typed: they come from the space, so a "wrong height" is a space (roof profile) question, not a placement one.
- **Bedroom body layout** (`generators/bedroom`, right panel `renderBedroom`): the body is five regions — `boot` (wall to wall, floor → `bootHeight`; made of three boards `BOOT_DECK` / `BOOT_BACK` / `BOOT_FRONT`), `wardrobeL` / `wardrobeR` (side wall → `wardrobeWidth`, boot deck → roof; Style 1 also `WARD_L/R_FIXED` + `WARD_L/R_DOOR`; nook `WARD_L/R_KICK` + `WARD_L/R_FLOOR` + `WARD_L/R_NOOK` + `WARD_L/R_DOOR`), `opening` (a void between them, boot deck → `ohcBottom`) and `ohc` (above the opening, to the roof). Symmetric by rule: one `wardrobeWidth` moves both faces. The opening must take the **bed frame** (`bedFrame`, `queen` = `BED_FRAME_QUEEN_WIDTH_MM` 1508): the wardrobes stop at `(W − 1508) / 2`. Wardrobe fronts: `style` `style1` (default) | `nook` (door over an open nook). Style 1 stores/drags `fixedPanelTop` (the split); nook does not drag a wardrobe bottom: it is `bootHeight` + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197 + `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_HEIGHT_MM` 204 (Style 3: 799). The door starts there (no + 4), the shelf (carcass 15, `WARDROBE_NOOK_SHELF_NOSE_GAP_MM` 22 short of the nose) sits on it, the wall strip stands on the shelf top and has no notch, the base is kick + floor (`WARDROBE_BASE_THICKNESS_MM` 18 each, floor top = boot + 197) and 595 → 799 on the room face is open — a "hole under the door" report in nook is the design, not a missing board. Each colour panel (`WARD_L/R_PANEL`) is cut through on the room edge between the floor top and that bottom: a fixed U, floor reach `nook.cut.floorY` 611.3, crest at `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_UPPER_CENTER_Y_MM` 502.775, arcs `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_UPPER_RADIUS_MM` 100 and `WARDROBE_NOOK_CUT_LOWER_RADIUS_MM` 703.374. Raising the boot moves the whole U; its height and depth stay put. A "cut missing" report on a nook colour panel is this outline, and there is no `nookShelfBottom` boundary to drag. `ledGroove` (default true; panel checkbox "LED channels", `bedroom.layout.set` with `key: "ledGroove"`, `how: "toggle"`) adds `tgroove` features `for: "led"` on face A of every T3 (`*_LED_MAIN` 14.5 × 6.5 full width, back wall `LED_T3_T1_GAP_MM` 0.5 in front of T1's front → Style 3 v 20 → 34.5; `*_LED_BRANCH_1/2` 20 wide (`LED_T3_BRANCH_WIDTH_MM`), centre `LED_T3_BRANCH_END_INSET_MM` 80 from each end of that board, from the main channel to the rear edge) and a `groove` `WARD_L/R_NOOK_LED` on face B of each nook shelf (centred, from `LED_NOOK_SHELF_FROM_ROOM_FACE_MM` 54 to the rear). A "channel in the wrong place" report is one of those rules; a "channel missing" report is the checkbox or a `generator.errors`/`warnings` line (`LED channel skipped: …`). The shelf depth is `depth − WARDROBE_NOOK_SHELF_NOSE_GAP_MM` 22, or less when the roof meets the shelf top first (`nook shelf stops at N` warning) — a "shelf shorter than the nose" report on a low nose is that, and a `generator.errors` line `the roof comes down to N at the nook shelf's back` was the old refusal (seq 24, 2026-09-23: roof 786.6 at y 748, shelf top 814, body depth 770). 3D draws every groove as a pocket, also on outlined boards (`boardGeom.js pocketGeometry`; touching T rectangles are merged into one hole); wardrobe floor = `bootHeight` + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197; door = split + `WARDROBE_DOOR_CLEARANCE_MM` 4 → T3 top; door hangs at y −`doorPanelThickness`…0 (envelope `frontPanelThickness` stays 0; front view is still the y = 0 elevation). `bedroom.layout.drag` (`key`, `side` ±1 for the mirrored pair, `from`, `to`, `changed`, `where: "front view"` — one boundary drag on the panel's front elevation, one undo step) / `bedroom.layout.set` (`key` incl. `bedFrame` / `style` / `fixedPanelTop` / `nookShelfBottom` / `ledGroove` / `ohcZones`, `from`, `to`, `how: "type" | "select"`, `clamped` = the `{min, max}` the other values left when the typed number was cut back, `changed`). `ohcZones` is 2 or 3 up-flap bays whose widths sum to the opening; a drag with `key: "ohcZone"` moves one centreline (`to` is the resulting zone list). The same layout numbers are the orange bars in 3D (standoff ~20 so they sit in front of the 16 mm doors): `handle.start` / `handle.end` with `handle: "divider"` carry `key` and the resulting `value` (index 0 boot deck, 1 overhead bottom, 2 / 3 wardrobe faces, 4 / 5 Style 1 fixed-panel split (nook has no wardrobe-bottom bar), then one bar per overhead bay boundary). Middle overhead boards are `OHC_BP` / `OHC_T3` / `OHC_D*` / `OHC_FP*`. `ohcBottom` is the door underside; the bottom panel is 30 above it and is cut 18 mm past the roof (`OHC_BP_OVERSIZE_MM`) — a "panel too deep" report is that allowance, not the uprights. No T4. A "wrong hinge" on an up flap is `OHC_HINGE_FROM_SIDE_2_MM` 150 (two bays, from the side-panel outer face and the centre divider) or `OHC_HINGE_FROM_SIDE_3_MM` 100 (three bays, from the door edge), 22.5 from the top. `select` carries `region` (`boot` | `wardrobeL` | `wardrobeR` | `opening` | `ohc`) when a region was picked in the front view or by a second 3D click — the body is still the selected object; `board` / `face` when a board was picked (`BOOT_*`, `WARD_L/R_STRIP` carcass wall strips (175 deep, boot deck, same top as the colour panel; Style 1 through notch y 75 → 175 for the shelf), `WARD_L/R_SHELF` Style 1 carcass shelves (10 above the wardrobe floor, tongue into the colour panel), `WARD_L/R_PANEL` colour panels, `WARD_L/R_T3`, `T2`, `T1`, `WARD_L/R_FIXED`, `WARD_L/R_DOOR`, nook `WARD_L/R_KICK` / `WARD_L/R_FLOOR` / `WARD_L/R_NOOK`). The wardrobe top hangs on one Y (`WARDROBE_T2_BACK_MM` 66) and the roof there: `layout.top` in the result gives `seat`, `t3Top`, `roofAtT2`, `t2Height`; `layout.front` gives Style 1 `floorTop` / `fixedPanelTop` / `doorBottom` / `doorTop` / `clearance`. A "T3 / colour panel step at the wrong height" report is the roof profile at y 66 (the space) or `WARDROBE_T2_HEIGHT_MM`, not a layout param. A "wrong door height / split" is `fixedPanelTop` (Style 1) / `nookShelfBottom` (nook) or T3 top; a "wrong hinge cup" is `WARDROBE_HINGE_*` (Ø35 × 12, 100 from each end, 22.5 from the wall-side edge, on door face A). `T1` is 20 above the roof on purpose (`WARDROBE_T1_OVERSIZE_MM`) and the generator says so in `validation.warnings`. A "wrong layout" report is the last `bedroom.layout.*` (or `handle.end` with `key`); the limits are `layoutLimits()` in the generator (`generators/bedroom/rules.json` minimums), and `generator.errors` names the one that was crossed ("the wardrobes leave only N mm between them — the queen bed frame needs 1508", "the fixed panel top N leaves only …"). Width / depth / roof are never typed here: they come from the space and the nose placement.
- `bedbox.arm` / `bedbox.blocked` / `bedbox.typein` / `bedbox.finish` / `bedbox.cancel` — Bed Box placement (needs the Bedroom body: `blocked` = no body). The box stands in the body's opening and runs into the room: `W` = the body's bed frame width (queen 1508), `H` = the body's `bootHeight` — both read from the body (`arm` carries `from`), never typed or dragged; only the length `D` is chosen (default `LENGTH_DEFAULT_MM` 979 in `generators/bedBox/rules.json`). `arm` carries `editId` (non-null = re-sizing the existing one), `bodyId`, `bodyDepth` and the starting `W/D/H`; `finish` carries `how`, `W`, `D`, `H`, `locked` (`{D}`), `snap`, `clamped` (`back wall` / `minimum N` / a cabinet id in the bed's lane), `edited`, `changed`, `pose` (`rotZ 180`, `x = centre + W/2`, `y = bodyDepth + D`). The bed box is 12 boards (`SIDE_L/R`, `END`, `DIVIDER`, `RAIL_L/R_LOW/HIGH`, `RAIL_END/BODY_LOW/HIGH`, all 18 stock; notches and the 1 mm oversize / gaps are `generators/bedBox/rules.json`). A "wrong bed width / height" report is a body question (`bedroom.layout.*`: bed frame / boot height), a "wrong notch / rail" report is a bedBox rule; an "off-centre bed" is a space-width or body question. `attach()` re-reads W / H whenever the body changes.
- `plane.arm` / `plane.blocked` / `plane.face` / `plane.typein` / `plane.finish` / `plane.cancel` / `plane.add` / `plane.remove` — construction plane: click a wall or cabinet face, offset a parallel copy into the room (`dir` of the source face). `face` carries `axis/value/dir/label/source/room`; `finish` / `add` carry `id`, `axis`, `value` (world), `offset`, `from` `{source, label}`. The plane is world-fixed (does not follow the source). Its outline vertices (plane ∩ space) are snap points. `blocked` = no space, or the face has no room outward.
- `bedside.arm` / `bedside.height` / `bedside.blocked` / `bedside.finish` / `bedside.cancel` — Bed Side Table placement (needs the Bedroom body; `blocked` = no body). North-south places **two** tables, one against each wall. Two clicks: first drag is the height (`bedside.height` on the click that locks it; `snap: "fixed panel bottom"` when H landed on the underside of the wardrobe fixed panel, boot height + `WARDROBE_FLOOR_RAISE_MM` 197), then the depth into the room. `arm` carries `W` (the wardrobe width), `H`, `snapH`, `D` (default 145). `finish` carries `pair: true` and the left table's id. `bedside.shelf` (`from`, `to`, the middle shelf centreline) / `bedside.zone` (`index` 0 lower / 1 upper, `type` `drawer` | `left_door` | `right_door` in that table's local frame; the other table stores the mirrored hand). The orange bar is `handle: "divider"`, `key: "shelfCenter"`. No back panel. Shelf tongues go through the sides (to the outer face) over the middle third of the depth; each side has a through slot per shelf, 1 mm taller than the shelf and 5 mm longer than the tongue at each end (`ROUTER_DIAMETER_MM` 10) — `SLOT_SHELF_MID` is a hole, the top / bottom slots are outline notches. The bed-side skin is `SHOW` (door stock, outside the carcass under the colour panel; carcass + `SHOW` = wardrobe width). Fronts cover the whole table with one `clearance` at every edge and between them. A nook shelf is not part of this module.
- `rail.group.open` — a rail group's flyout (Bedroom → Body / Bed Box / Bed Side Table) opened on hover or click.
- `resize.start` / `resize.face` / `resize.blocked` / `resize.drag` / `resize.end` / `resize.exit` — the Resize command (S, command bar). Click a face of any module's box (`face` `{axis, dir, label}` in **cabinet-local** axes: `x-` left side, `x+` right side, `y-` front / room face, `y+` back, `z+` top, `z-` bottom); an arrow appears on it; one drag = one undo step, the opposite face stays. `blocked` `reason: "face is fixed"` with `pullable` = the module's `resizeFaces` (floor sides never move; overhead: sides, front, bottom; bedroom body / bed box: the room face only; bed side table: room face + top; lounge: none). `end` carries `from` / `to` `{envelope, pose, zones}` and `stopped` (`minimum N`, `a zone at its minimum`, `the space boundary`, a cabinet / wall id, or the generator error that refused the size — the last good size is kept). Zone rules (`modules.js resizeFace`, `resizeStack`): kitchen side face → a new column once the pull reaches 150 (`left_door` on the left face, `right_door` on the right; a new top zone is `left_door`), a push trims the end column and one under 150 merges into its neighbour; kitchen top → the same on each column's top zone (60); overhead sides → the same with `up_flap` bays (150); small cabinet top → only the top zone, one under 60 merges below; tall top → every zone keeps its share (`fitTallCabinetHeight`); bed side table top → the upper zone (the shelf stays until `attach` pushes it down). A "wrong zone after resize" report is the last `resize.end` `from.zones` → `to.zones`.
- `board.visibility` (`id`, `boards` role ids, `visible`, `hidden` the cabinet's remaining hidden list) — show or hide boards in the 3D view. Stored on `cabinet.hidden` and saved with the job; a hidden board stays hidden until it is shown again. `V` toggles the selected board, or every board of the module when the module itself is selected (a face selection toggles its board). The browser eye toggles one board and does not change the selection. Right-click a module row: Show all boards / Hide all boards.
- `cabinet.add` / `cabinet.remove` / `cabinet.params` / `cabinet.pose` / `select` / `key.delete` / `key.rotate`. `select` carries `id` (cabinet / wall / plane or null) plus `board` (role id such as `BP`) and `face` (`A` / `B` / `E<i>`) when the Job tree, a second 3D click or the drawer narrowed the selection to a board / face inside that cabinet — the cabinet is still the selected object; a "clicked the wrong board / face" report is the last `select` with a non-null `board`.
- **Partition walls / floor plan** (`renderer/floorplan.js`, `walls.js`): `floorplan.open` (`from` button|panel, counts) / `floorplan.close` (`how` button|esc|no space) / `floorplan.tool` (`tool` wall|door|null, `type` showerDoor|slidingDoor for a door) / `floorplan.select` (`id`, `kind` wall|cabinet|null) / `floorplan.blocked` / `floorplan.cancel` (a right-click with nothing to cancel). Drawing (one tool, three steps pt1 → pt2 → offset): `wall.draw.point` (`step` pt1|pt2, `edge` id such as `space:2` or `wall-1:y-#1`, `line` what the user sees — `Left wall` or the partition id, `u`, `length`, `snap` feature|align|grid|locked|`far end`, `alignTo` the partition / cabinet whose centre line / side the point lines up with, `candidates` the lines through the first point, `junction` `{wall, segments}` when the first point sits on a partition standing on the line — both segments beside it stay candidates and the pull direction picks one, `how` click|enter), `wall.draw.typein` (`step`, `value`, `locked`; `how: "tab"` when Tab opened the field), `wall.draw.blocked` (`issues` — e.g. `overlaps cab-1`, `free-standing: neither end rests on a wall`, `outside the space`, or `reason` `shorter than 50`; the wall that was refused), `wall.draw.cancel` (`step`, `reason` esc|right-click|tool off|close), `wall.draw.finish` (`id`, `wall` `{axis, at, u0, u1, side}`, `how` click|enter, `from` the source — a space wall label or the partition id, `edge` the face actually offset from (`wall-1:y-` = its nose face), `offset`, `alignedOffset` partition id when the offset snapped to its centre line, `clamped`, `length` (the board), `drawn` `{u0, u1}` (the picked span, centre line to centre line), `trimmed` `{lo, hi}` — `{id, from, to, by}` per end that was drawn into a partition and pulled back onto its face (a "wall is 9 short" report is this: the point was on wall-1's centre line, the board stops on its face), `anchors` `{lo, hi}` — an end resting ON another partition's face (never inside: no physical overlap, ever). Job level: `wall.add` (the record + the same meta) / `wall.remove` (`id`, `wall`); `key.delete` carries `where: "floorplan"` when pressed on the sheet (with `wallId` when it removed a door, not the wall). **Shower doors** (`floorplan.tool` `door`): `opening.draw.end` (`wallId`, `from` lo|hi, `endU`), `opening.draw.start` (`u`, `offset` from that end, `snap`, `alignTo`), `opening.draw.width` (`u`, `width`, `snap`), `opening.draw.typein`, `opening.draw.clearance` (card opened with the remembered `bottom` / `top`), `opening.draw.blocked` (`step`, `issues` e.g. `new door: overlaps op-1`, `reason` `narrower than 50`, or `no walls`), `opening.draw.cancel` (`step`, `reason` esc|right-click|card cancel|tool off|close), `opening.draw.finish` (`wallId`, `id`, `from`, `offset`, `width`, `bottom`, `top`, `how` enter|ok). Job level: `opening.add` / `opening.set` (`patch`, resulting `opening`) / `opening.remove`. A "door in the wrong place" report: `opening.draw.start` says which end and how far, `opening.draw.width` the width; the record is on the wall (`wall.remove` carries the whole wall incl. `openings`). **Sliding doors** (`floorplan.tool` `door` with `type: "slidingDoor"`; every `opening.draw.*` entry carries `type`): same `end` / `start` / `width` steps, then `opening.draw.side` (`side` ±1 along the wall's axis, `label` front|back|left side…, `pelmet` `{length, stoppedBy: {lo, hi}}` — what the pelmet ran into on that side: a space wall label or a partition id), `opening.draw.clearance` carries `top`, `overlap`, `doorHeight`, `opening.draw.blocked` at `step: "side"` carries the refused `side` and `issues` (`no room to slide open (needs W beside the door leaf)` — W is the hole width minus 100: the open leaf may stick out 100 past the pelmet; only more than that is refused — W is the hole width minus 100: the open leaf may stick out 100 past the pelmet; only more than that is refused, `door leaf H high runs into the ceiling (max …)`, `leaf hits cab-1`, `pelmet hits cab-2`, `door leaf runs past wall-2`), `opening.draw.finish` adds `side`, `sideLabel`, `overlap`, `doorHeight`, `leaf` `{length, z0, z1}`, `pelmet` `{length, height, underside, stoppedBy}`, `cover` (leaf top − pelmet underside; positive = the pelmet hides the track) and `warnings` (`pelmet stops N above the leaf top — the track shows`, a soft warning, never a refusal). The hole's `bottom` is always 0. Leaf / pelmet positions are not stored — they follow `stock.partition.thickness` (leaf 20 clear of the wall face, pelmet 20 clear of the leaf) and the space (pelmet top = roof − ceiling clearance), so a "pelmet too short" report is about the partition it stopped at (`stoppedBy`), and a "track shows" report is `cover` < 0 (leaf top = 15 + `doorHeight`; pelmet underside = wall top − `top`). A "wrong wall" report is normally the last `wall.draw.finish`: `at` is the reference face (`offset` from `from`), `side` the way the thickness grows; the two `wall.draw.point` entries before it say which edge and which snap fixed each end; thickness / height are not in the record — they follow `stock.partition` (`thickness`, `floorClearance`, `ceilingClearance`) in `space.define` / `materials.set`.
- `handle.start` / `handle.end` — W/D/H face or `divider` drags with envelope before/after, zones and pose. Handles are hidden while a board / face inside the cabinet is selected. `handle.arm` (`id`, `handle`, `on`, `how: "esc"` when Esc removed it) — an **on-demand** handle (`mod.handlesOnDemand`, today the Bed Box length `D`): the panel's ⇕ button next to the field shows a double arrow just outside that face in 3D; dragging it logs the usual `handle.start` / `handle.end`; it goes on Esc, on a selection change or when pressed again.
- `space.dialog.open` / `space.dialog.close` / `space.dialog.kind` / `space.dialog.choice` / `space.define` — `values` carries the whole form (vehicle: `width`, `rearDepth`, `height`, `frontMode`, `front.points` / `front.dxf.points` as `[y, z]` mm). `space.define` / dialog open+close also carry job-level `finish` (`carcass` White Stipple; `door.series` acrylic|hpl; `door.mode` one|two; `door.colors` `[{id, series, name}]`) and `stock` (`carcass` / `partition` / `door` thicknesses). `space.dialog.choice` on the catalogue uses `key` `finish.door.series` or `finish.door.mode`.
- `materials.set` — the job catalogue was written (`finish`, `stock`). Existing cabinets keep the thicknesses they already copied; new ones inherit.
- **Door colour in 3D**: the generators write the cabinet's `doorColorName` (default `Gloss White`) into `finish.colour` on each colour face — door / flap fronts' B face (−Y), tall `T1` / `B1`, overhead `T1`, kitchen `B1`, bedroom colour panels and fronts, small-cabinet fronts, bed-side `SHOW`. The 3D view paints the whole of that board with one flat material from `renderer/doorSwatches.js` (StyleLite screen values; matte / gloss / metallic flakes). No environment reflection in this view. Carcass boards are White Stipple (`renderer/carcassFinish.js`): warm white `F3F3F0` with an even medium stipple, not the old beige. `colour.unknown` (`name`) = a door-stock colour face whose name has no swatch (HPL today): it shows grey. A "wrong colour" report is the cabinet's `doorColorName` in `cabinet.params` or the swatch table, never the geometry. A "carcass looks beige / smooth" report is the stipple material not applied.
- `materials.default.set` / `materials.default.reset` — "Set current as default" for colours + board stocks: `ok`, `path` (settings.json), `error`, `finish`, `stock`.
- `space.dxf.import` — `ok`, `path`, `points`, `chains`, `units`, `scale`, `counts` per entity type, `warnings`, `range` `{y, z}`; or `error`.
- `space.default.set` / `space.default.reset` — "Set current as default" for a space kind: `ok`, `path` (settings.json), `error`, `values`.
- `settings.loaded` / `settings.corrupt` / `settings.read.failed` / `settings.write.failed` — user settings file (`<userData>/settings.json`); a corrupt file falls back to built-in defaults and is left in place.
- Placement / move `clamped` may name `roof` (the sloped nose of a vehicle) instead of `ceiling`.
- `file.new` / `file.open` / `file.open.failed` / `file.save`, `undo` / `redo`, `view`.
- **Generator bench** (`docs/bench-spec.md`; separate window, developer tool): `bench.open` (`module`, `preset`, `from` rail|cabinet|env|tab, `custom`), `bench.tab`, `bench.preset`, `bench.preset.save`, `bench.param` (`key`, `from`, `to`), `bench.select` (`what` board|face|point|joint|key, `id`, `key` / `keys`, `value`, `formula`), `bench.tryout` (`key`, `formula`, `expr`, `from`, `to` — a what-if on one value; nothing was changed), `bench.rule.set` (`name`, `from`, `to`, **`reason`**, `affected` keys, `boards`, `path` — rules.json **was** rewritten), `bench.rebuild` (`ok`, `ms`), `bench.pin` (`preset`, `what` board|all, `id`, `count`, `faces`), `bench.report` (`path` of `logs/bench/<time>-<module>.md`, `selection`, `note`), `bench.board.open` / `bench.board.frame`, `bench.view`, `bench.explode` (`mode` assembly|radial, `factor` slider 0..1, `step` null = all boards follow the slider | n = n boards in, `of`, `board` = the one that just went in, `order` = the inferred assembly order, `how` slider|step|key|chip|panel|mode — display only: groups move, boards never change; a "wrong direction / wrong order" report is about `renderer/bench/explode.js` reading `joints` + face features `for`, not about the generator).
  - When asked to fix a generator dimension: read the newest `logs/bench/*.md` first — it names the board / point, its provenance chain (`value = formula (terms)`), what was tried and what the user expects. Then `bench.rule.set` entries: the `reason` is the user's design intent; keep it. Pinned values (`generators/<module>/presets.json`) are the protected numbers; run the generator test after any change.
- `generator.errors` — generator `validation.errors` for a cabinet.
- `error` / `console.error` — with stack.

## Rules

- Quote the relevant entries (seq + kind + the numbers) when explaining a cause.
- If the files are missing or the last entry is older than the user's report, say so; do not invent events.
- Log payloads are plain JSON; never contain Three.js objects.
